---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Legal Framework of Bail" activePage="legal-framework">

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero-tag">§ 02 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Legal Framework of Bail</div>
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div>
          <h1>The Law on <span class="ital">Bail</span><br/>in Malaysia</h1>
          <p class="hero-sub">Understanding Bail: Your Rights, The Rules, and The Exceptions — the statutory framework that governs pre-trial release under Malaysian criminal justice.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="crest" aria-label="Framework record">
          <dl>
            <div class="crest-row"><dt>Volume</dt><dd>I</dd></div>
            <div class="crest-row"><dt>Section</dt><dd>§ 02</dd></div>
            <div class="crest-row"><dt>Type</dt><dd>Framework</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <div class="crest-divider"></div>
          <div class="crest-num">03<small>Offence Categories</small></div>
          <div class="crest-divider"></div>
          <dl>
            <div class="crest-row"><dt>Subject</dt><dd>SOSMA</dd></div>
            <div class="crest-row"><dt>Reform</dt><dd>Yes</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Statutory Framework -->
  <section class="article">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="article-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">§ 02.1 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Framework</div>
          <h2 style="margin:0;">The Statutory<br/>Framework</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p style="font-size:20px; color:var(--ink-soft); margin:0 0 18px;">The Types of Bail Category Under Offences — the Criminal Procedure Code arranges offences along a spectrum of judicial discretion.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="offence-grid">
        <article class="offence-cell">
          <span class="badge">Category I</span>
          <h3>Bailable Offence</h3>
          <p>Less severe offence where the accused is entitled to bail as a matter of right. The court cannot deny bail if the accused is ready to furnish it.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="offence-cell">
          <span class="badge">Category II</span>
          <h3>Non&#8209;Bailable Offence</h3>
          <p>More serious offence where granting bail is at the discretion of the court. The judge will weigh factors like flight risk, the severity of the charge, and likelihood of tampering with witnesses.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="offence-cell">
          <span class="badge">Category III</span>
          <h3>Unbailable Offence</h3>
          <p>Offences carrying the death penalty or life imprisonment generally cannot be granted bail.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SOSMA -->
  <section class="article">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="article-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">§ 02.2 &nbsp;·&nbsp; SOSMA</div>
          <h2 style="margin:0;">Security Offences<br/>(Special Measures)<br/>Act 2012</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="measure">
          <p>The Security Offences (Special Measures) Act 2012 (SOSMA) is a strict security law that severely limits the right to bail.</p>

          <div class="sosma-grid">
            <div class="sosma-card">
              <div class="sosma-label">The General Rule</div>
              <p>It completely bans judges from granting pre-trial bail for security offences, forcing accused individuals to stay in prison until their trial ends.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="sosma-card">
              <div class="sosma-label">The Exceptions</div>
              <p>The law only allows narrow exceptions for women, youths under 18, and the dangerously ill. Even then, they face harsh conditions like electronic ankle trackers.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="sosma-card">
              <div class="sosma-label">Judicial Independence</div>
              <p>Recently, the courts struck down a harsh SOSMA rule that forced individuals to stay locked up even after being found innocent — ruling that acquitted individuals must be freed immediately.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <blockquote class="pull-large">"The absolute ban has caused a major constitutional tug-of-war over the separation of powers between Parliament and the courts."</blockquote>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Reform Opinion -->
  <section class="article">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="article-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">§ 02.3 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Opinion</div>
          <h2 style="margin:0;">A Pathway<br/>for Reform</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="measure">
          <p>The article draws on international jurisprudence to propose a clear pathway for reforming Malaysia's strict bail laws, focusing on how courts can prevent prolonged pre-trial detention from becoming an unconstitutional punishment.</p>
          <p>It argues that Malaysia should adopt the <em>Najeeb principle</em> for its SOSMA regime. Although SOSMA is heavily protected by Article 149 of the Federal Constitution, keeping an unconvicted person locked up indefinitely directly violates the fundamental right to life and liberty under Article 5(1).</p>
          <p>When security trials become excessively delayed, statutory prohibitions must yield to the constitutional right to a speedy trial — to ensure the State cannot transform pre-trial detention into unauthorised punishment.</p>
          <div class="case-row">
            <article class="case-cell">
              <p class="case-name">Charles v. Attorney General</p>
              <p class="case-loc">Trinidad &amp; Tobago</p>
              <p>Highlighted how total bail bans damage the separation of powers between the legislature and the judiciary.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="case-cell">
              <p class="case-name">Union of India v. K.A. Najeeb</p>
              <p class="case-loc">Supreme Court of India</p>
              <p>Constitutional courts retain inherent power to bypass strict statutory bail bans where trial is unreasonably delayed.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
          <p style="margin-top:30px; font-style:italic; color:var(--muted); font-size:16px;">See also: <strong>PP v. Dato' Mat Safuan</strong> — an early Malaysian authority supporting the constitutional right to a speedy trial.</p>
          <a class="article-link" href="/legal-foundation" style="margin-top:30px;">
            <span>
              <span class="label">Deep dive</span>
              <strong>Read the full academic article</strong>
            </span>
            <svg class="arrow" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <path d="M7 17 17 7"/><path d="M8 7h9v9"/>
            </svg>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>

<style>
.article {
  padding: 80px 0;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
}
.article:last-of-type { border-bottom: 0; }
.article-grid {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr; gap: 80px;
}
.article-grid p { margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--ink-soft); }
.article-grid p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

.offence-grid {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  gap: 1px; background: var(--rule); border: 1px solid var(--rule); margin-top: 40px;
}
.offence-cell {
  background: var(--bg); padding: 40px 30px 36px; display: flex; flex-direction: column;
}
.offence-cell .badge {
  font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: .22em;
  text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--gold-deep);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 5px 10px;
  align-self: flex-start; margin-bottom: 18px;
}
.offence-cell h3 { font-size: 24px; margin: 0 0 14px; font-weight: 500; }
.offence-cell p { margin: 0; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft); }

.sosma-grid {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  gap: 1px; background: var(--rule); border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  margin: 24px 0 28px;
}
.sosma-card { background: var(--bg); padding: 24px 28px; }
.sosma-label {
  font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: .22em;
  text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--gold-deep);
  margin-bottom: 10px;
}
.sosma-card p { margin: 0; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft); }

.case-row {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  gap: 1px; background: var(--rule); border: 1px solid var(--rule); margin-top: 30px;
}
.case-cell { background: var(--bg); padding: 30px 28px; }
.case-cell .case-name { font-family: var(--serif); font-style: italic; font-size: 20px; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 8px; }
.case-cell .case-loc {
  font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: .2em;
  text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 14px;
}
.case-cell p { margin: 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft); }

.article-link {
  display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px;
  border: 1px solid var(--ink); padding: 18px 22px;
  text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink);
  font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 13px;
  background: var(--surface);
  transition: all .15s ease;
  max-width: 520px;
}
.article-link:hover { background: var(--ink); color: var(--bg); }
.article-link .label {
  font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: .22em; text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--muted); display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;
}
.article-link:hover .label { color: rgba(246,242,232,.6); }
.article-link strong { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 18px; font-style: italic; font-weight: 500; }
.article-link .arrow { margin-left: auto; }

@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .article-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 36px; }
  .offence-grid, .case-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .article { padding: 60px 0; }
}
</style>
